  "answer": "The study confirmed 4 of the 5 cases it was asked for: 048b974e at 0.8x / \u22121 s (S3), 07981e6a at 1.4x / +1 s (S8), 0e002edd at 1.4x / +0.5 s (S17) and 1bbe02fc at 0.8x / +2 s (S29). The fifth candidate, 18f8dbd6 at 1.4x / \u22120.5 s (S24), failed every time but had too few repeats to clear the bar. None of these failures involved the pedestrian. Every failure the triage read was the policy leaving its route. It veered left against a RIGHT route command, drifted into the lane or parking lane on its left, or left the road, and then hit a van, a parked car or another vehicle. So the study did not find any speed and step-out time combination that makes the policy hit the pedestrian. In 048b974e, 1bbe02fc and 07981e6a, failures happened at almost every setting tried, so they look like scene-level route errors rather than effects of speed or timing. Only 0e002edd and 18f8dbd6 hint that 1.4x speed matters, and each rests on one setting with a few runs.",
